        modelAnswer: "(a) Relief & The Himalayas:\nThe towering Himalayas act as an impenetrable climatic barrier. In winter, they shield the subcontinent from bitterly cold Arctic-Siberian winds, keeping northern India warmer than other locations at the same latitude. In summer, they trap moisture-bearing southwest monsoon winds, causing heavy orographic rainfall across the plains.\n\n(b) Altitude:\nAs altitude increases, the atmosphere becomes less dense and temperature decreases (lapse rate of ~6.5°C per 1,000 m). Consequently, hill stations like Shimla and Ooty remain cool even during peak summer.\n\n(c) Indian Ocean Proximity:\nPeninsular India is bounded by water on three sides. The moderating maritime influence prevents extreme diurnal and seasonal temperature variations, ensuring mild coastal climates without harsh winters.",
        stepMarking: [{"step":"1 Mark for Himalayan double shield (blocking Siberian freeze & trapping monsoons)","marks":"1 Mark"},{"step":"1 Mark for altitude lapse rate cooling effect","marks":"1 Mark"},{"step":"1 Mark for maritime moderation of coastal peninsula","marks":"1 Mark"}],
