        questionText: "Why is the Indian Judiciary described as an \"independent and integrated\" institution? How does it exercise Judicial Review?",

        modelAnswer: "1. Independent Judiciary:\nJudges of the Supreme Court and High Courts are appointed by the President upon consultation with senior judges (Collegium). Once appointed, they cannot be removed except by a nearly impossible parliamentary impeachment (two-thirds majority in both houses). Salaries cannot be altered to their disadvantage, ensuring fearlessness.\n\n2. Integrated Hierarchy:\nThe Supreme Court stands at the apex; its decisions are binding on all High Courts and subordinate district courts throughout India.\n\n3. Judicial Review:\nThe Supreme Court and High Courts have the constitutional power to examine the legislative enactments and executive orders. If any law violates the fundamental provisions or the \"Basic Structure\" of the Constitution, the Judiciary declares it unconstitutional and null and void.",
        stepMarking: [{"step":"1 Mark for independence (appointment and removal safeguards)","marks":"1 Mark"},{"step":"1 Mark for integrated single court hierarchy","marks":"1 Mark"},{"step":"1 Mark for Judicial Review definition (striking down unconstitutional laws)","marks":"1 Mark"}],
