        modelAnswer: "(a) Causes of the French Revolution:\n1. Social Inequality: Society was rigidly divided into Three Estates. The First and Second Estates (clergy and nobility) enjoyed birth exemptions from taxation, held top government posts, and extracted feudal dues. The Third Estate carried 100% of the financial burden.\n2. Financial Bankruptcy: Wars (including funding the American War of Independence) burdened France with over 2 billion livres of debt. Extravagant court expenditure at Versailles drained the royal treasury.\n3. Subsistence Crisis: Bad weather in 1788–89 ruined wheat harvests, causing bread prices to soar. Starving peasants and bread rioters marched on Paris.\n4. Rise of Intellectual Bourgeoisie: Philosophers like Montesquieu (*The Spirit of the Laws*), Rousseau (*The Social Contract*), and John Locke (*Two Treatises of Government*) challenged divine right of kings, advocating popular sovereignty and separation of powers.\n5. Immediate Catalyst: On 14 July 1789, rumors spread that the King had ordered the army to fire on citizens. A people's militia stormed and demolished the royal fortress-prison of the Bastille, symbolizing the overthrow of tyrannical absolutism.\n\n(b) Legacy of the Declaration of Rights of Man:\n- It declared that \"men are born and remain free and equal in rights.\"\n- Enshrined natural and inalienable rights: Liberty, Property, Security, and Resistance to Oppression.\n- Established equality before the law and freedom of speech and press.\n- This document inspired anti-colonial liberation struggles across Europe, Latin America, and India (e.g., Tipu Sultan and Raja Rammohan Roy).",
        stepMarking: [{"step":"3 Marks for comprehensive breakdown of social, financial, and philosophical causes","marks":"3 Marks"},{"step":"2 Marks for the Declaration of Rights of Man principles and global legacy","marks":"2 Marks"}],
        examinerTip: "Cite specific philosophers (Locke, Rousseau, Montesquieu) and the debt amount (2 billion livres).",
