        marks: 4,
        topic: "Case Study (History): Women in the French Revolution",
        questionText: "Read the historical excerpt and answer the questions:\nFrom the very beginning, women were active participants in the events which brought about so many important changes in French society. Most women of the third estate had to work for a living as seamstresses, laundresses, or domestic servants. They did not have access to education or job training. In order to discuss and voice their interests, women started their own political clubs and newspapers. About sixty women's clubs came up in different French cities. The Society of Revolutionary and Republican Women was the most famous of them. One of their main demands was that women enjoy the same political rights as men. The Constitution of 1791, however, reduced them to passive citizens with no voting rights.\n\nQuestions:\n(a) Which was the most famous political club organized by French women? (1 Mark)\n(b) How did the Constitution of 1791 disappoint French women? (1 Mark)\n(c) State two legal reforms introduced by the revolutionary government to improve the condition of women in France. In which year did French women finally win the right to vote? (2 Marks)",

        modelAnswer: "(a) The most famous club was \"The Society of Revolutionary and Republican Women\".\n\n(b) The Constitution of 1791 reduced women to \"passive citizens\" without voting rights or the right to hold political office.\n\n(c) Legal Reforms & Enfranchisement:\n1. Schooling was made compulsory for all girls.\n2. Fathers could no longer force daughters into marriages against their will; marriage was made into a civil contract registered under civil law.\n3. Divorce was made legal and could be applied for by both men and women.\n- Year of Voting Rights: French women finally won the right to vote in 1946 (after World War II).",
        stepMarking: [{"step":"Society of Revolutionary and Republican Women identified","marks":"1 Mark"},{"step":"Passive citizen classification explained","marks":"1 Mark"},{"step":"Compulsory schooling/divorce laws + Year 1946","marks":"2 Marks"}],
        examinerTip: "Note the year 1946; women had to struggle for over 150 years after the 1789 revolution to get voting rights.",
        commonPitfall: "Writing that women got voting rights during the 1789 revolution."
