        modelAnswer: "(a) Safeguards Making Indian Elections Democratic:\n1. Universal Adult Suffrage: Every citizen aged 18 and above has the right to vote without discrimination based on caste, religion, or gender, with one vote having one equal value.\n2. Independent Election Commission (EC): The EC enjoys vast autonomous constitutional powers: it enforces the Model Code of Conduct, deploys security forces, and censures ministers violating campaign rules.\n3. Universal Choice & Political Parties: Free registration of political parties gives voters genuine political alternatives and competitive campaign manifestos.\n4. Acceptance of Election Outcomes: Defeated ruling parties regularly and peacefully surrender power (an incumbent government loses half of the elections in India), proving that outcomes are genuinely decided by voters.\n\n(b) Challenges & Limitations Requiring Reform:\n1. Money Power & Criminalization: Wealthy candidates and major parties enjoy unfair advantages over independent candidates. Furthermore, candidates with serious criminal antecedents often secure party tickets due to electoral clout.\n2. Dynastic Politics: Many political parties lack internal organizational democracy, concentrating party leadership and candidate tickets within family dynasties.",
