        modelAnswer: "(a) Tabular Comparison:\n| Parameter | Himalayan River System (e.g. Ganga, Indus) | Peninsular River System (e.g. Godavari, Krishna) |\n|---|---|---|\n| (i) Origin & Catchment | Snow-capped Himalayan glaciers; vast international catchment basins | Peninsular plateaus and Western Ghats; smaller regional catchments |\n| (ii) Water Regimes | Strictly Perennial (fed by snow-melt in summer and monsoon rains) | Non-perennial / Seasonal (dependent exclusively on monsoon rainfall) |\n| (iii) Valley Profile | Deep, steep V-shaped valleys and gorges (geologically young) | Broad, shallow, graded valleys (geologically ancient and mature) |\n| (iv) Course & Meanders | Long, meandering courses with floodplains, ox-bow lakes, and massive deltas | Shorter, straight, fixed courses with minimal meandering |\n| (v) Economic Use | Highly suitable for inland navigation, perennial canal irrigation, and hydro-power | Ideal for hydro-electric power generation due to rocky waterfalls; less suitable for canal navigation |\n\n(b) Eastward vs Westward Drainage Explanation:\n- Most Peninsular rivers (Godavari, Krishna, Mahanadi, Kaveri) flow eastward because the Peninsular plateau gently slopes from west to east towards the Bay of Bengal, forming fertile coastal deltas.\n- In contrast, the Narmada and Tapi flow westward into the Arabian Sea because they do not flow on the general plateau surface; instead, they flow through deep tectonic fault lines / rift valleys created by tectonic subsidence between the Vindhya and Satpura mountain ranges, forming estuaries rather than deltas.",
