        questionText: "Read the developmental economics report and answer the questions:\nAlong with government food intervention programmes, cooperative societies are playing a monumental role in food security in India, especially in the southern and western parts of the country. The cooperative societies set up shops to sell goods to poor people at lower prices. In Tamil Nadu, of all fair price shops running, around 94 percent are being run by the cooperatives. In Gujarat, Amul has brought about the White Revolution in the country by establishing a cooperative milk grid that eliminates middlemen and provides affordable milk to consumers while guaranteeing remunerative prices to small dairy farmers.\n\nQuestions:\n(a) What proportion of Fair Price Shops in Tamil Nadu are managed by cooperatives? (1 Mark)\n(b) How did Amul bring about the \"White Revolution\" in India? (1.5 Marks)\n(c) Mention any one cooperative initiative in Delhi that supplies milk and vegetables at controlled prices to consumers. (1.5 Marks)",

        modelAnswer: "(a) In Tamil Nadu, approximately 94% of all Fair Price Shops are managed by cooperative societies.\n\n(b) Amul's White Revolution:\nAmul established an organized network of rural dairy cooperative societies in Anand (Gujarat), pooling milk directly from millions of small and marginal farmers. By eliminating exploitative middlemen, setting up modern pasteurization and cold-storage processing plants, and distributing dairy products nationwide, Amul drastically increased national milk production (Operation Flood) and ensured fair, guaranteed incomes to rural households.\n\n(c) Initiative in Delhi:\nMother Dairy (established under Operation Flood by the National Dairy Development Board), which provides quality milk and fresh vegetables (under the \"Safal\" brand) at controlled, subsidized prices through thousands of community booths across Delhi-NCR.",
