        modelAnswer: "(a) Two-Plank Anti-Poverty Strategy:\n1. Promotion of Economic Growth: Accelerating overall GDP growth through industrialization, infrastructure, and services. High economic growth creates employment, generates tax revenues, and provides resources to invest in human capital.\n2. Targeted Anti-Poverty Programmes: Direct targeted welfare interventions for households that growth fails to reach quickly (e.g., MGNREGA for rural employment, PMGY for village health/schools, PDS for subsidized food grains).\n\n(b) Interstate Disparities & Explanations:\n1. High Poverty States:\n   - Bihar (33.7%) and Odisha (32.6%) remain the poorest states in India. Reasons include recurrent natural disasters (floods in Bihar, cyclones in Odisha), low agricultural productivity, high landlessness, and inadequate industrial infrastructure.\n2. Success States (Low Poverty):\n   - Punjab and Haryana: Successfully reduced poverty by achieving high agricultural growth rates through the Green Revolution (assured irrigation, HYV seeds, fertilizers).\n   - Kerala: Succeeded by investing heavily in human resource development (universal literacy, female education, and high-quality primary healthcare).\n   - West Bengal: Reduced rural poverty significantly through radical land reform measures (Operation Barga protecting tenant farmers).\n   - Tamil Nadu and Andhra Pradesh: Succeeded through the efficient universal implementation of the Public Distribution System (PDS) ensuring subsidized food to all vulnerable citizens.",
