        questionText: "Read the meteorological report and answer the questions:\nThe climate of India is strongly influenced by monsoon winds. Around the time of its arrival in early June, the normal rainfall increases suddenly and continues constantly for several days. This is known as the \"burst\" of the monsoon, and can be distinguished from pre-monsoon showers. By the first week of June, the monsoon reaches the southern tip of the Indian peninsula. Subsequently, it splits into two branches: the Arabian Sea branch and the Bay of Bengal branch. Towards late October and November, tropical depressions originate over the Andaman Sea and cross the eastern coast, causing devastating loss of life and property in the deltas of the Godavari, Krishna, and Kaveri.\n\nQuestions:\n(a) What is meant by the \"burst\" of the monsoon? (1 Mark)\n(b) Name the two branches into which the South-West Monsoon splits upon hitting the Indian peninsula. (1 Mark)\n(c) Why do the coastal deltas of the Godavari, Krishna, and Kaveri experience destructive tropical cyclones during the retreating monsoon season? (2 Marks)",

        modelAnswer: "(a) \"Burst\" of the Monsoon:\nThe sudden, dramatic increase in heavy torrential rainfall in early June that continues uninterrupted for several days, marking the formal arrival of the South-West Monsoon.\n\n(b) Two Branches:\n1. The Arabian Sea Branch\n2. The Bay of Bengal Branch.\n\n(c) Reason for Destructive Cyclones in Eastern Deltas:\nDuring October–November, the monsoon trough of low pressure shifts southwards over the Bay of Bengal. This seasonal shift triggers intense tropical depressions over the Andaman Sea. Driven by warm sea-surface temperatures, these depressions intensify into severe tropical cyclones that strike the eastern coast, causing storm surges, devastating floods, and severe destruction of property in the low-lying deltas of Godavari, Krishna, and Kaveri.",
